.\" robodr-deliverd(8). Source of truth: the USAGE text in
.\" crates/robodr-deliverd/src/cli.rs, the crate docs and
.\" docs/SECURE-DELIVERY.md (sections 4.2, 6 and 10). Keep them in sync.
.TH ROBODR\-DELIVERD 8 "2026-10-30" "RoboDR 0.1.0-alpha.1" "RoboDR Manual"
.SH NAME
robodr\-deliverd \- install verified RoboDR delivery packages on a unit, behind the robot's safety-state gate
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B robodr\-deliverd run \-\-config
.I FILE
.br
.B robodr\-deliverd once \-\-config
.I FILE
.br
.B robodr\-deliverd check\-config \-\-config
.I FILE
.br
.B robodr\-deliverd init\-anchor \-\-config
.I FILE
.B \-\-trust
.I TRUST.json
.br
.B robodr\-deliverd reanchor \-\-config
.I FILE
.B \-\-trust
.I TRUST.json
.RB [ \-\-key
.IR KEYFILE ]
.br
.B robodr\-deliverd rekey \-\-config
.I FILE
.B \-\-old\-key
.I KEYFILE
.B \-\-new\-key
.I KEYFILE
.br
.B robodr\-deliverd status \-\-config
.I FILE
.br
.B robodr\-deliverd confirm \-\-config
.I FILE
.B \-\-deployment
.I DIGEST
.B \-\-package
.I NAME
.br
.B robodr\-deliverd erase \-\-config
.I FILE
.B \-\-trigger
.I TRIGGER
.RB [ \-\-device
.IR DEV ]
.RB [ \-\-key
.IR KEYFILE ]
.br
.B robodr\-deliverd pull \-\-config
.I FILE
.br
.B robodr\-deliverd report \-\-config
.I FILE
.RB [ \-\-out
.IR FILE ]
.br
.B robodr\-deliverd lease \-\-config
.I FILE
.B \-\-lease
.I FILE
.br
.B robodr\-deliverd tamper \-\-socket
.I PATH
.RB [ \-\-trigger
.IR tamper | attestation_failed | auth_failures ]
.br
.B robodr\-deliverd safety\-state
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR FILE ]
.br
.B robodr\-deliverd verify\-minisign \-\-pubkey
.I FILE
.B \-\-file
.I FILE
.RB [ \-\-sig
.IR FILE ]
.br
.B robodr\-deliverd verify\-minisign \-\-self\-test
.br
.B robodr\-deliverd \-\-version
.SH DESCRIPTION
The on-unit delivery agent of RoboDR's secure delivery (Sprint 13b). It
installs packages (RPKs, format
.BR robodr\-pkg/1 )
of the kinds
.BR files ,
.BR config ,
.B deb
and
.B robodr\-rules
from
.B per-unit media packs
that an operator puts (copies or mounts) under the configured
.IR media_dir .
A media pack is a directory holding exactly
.I trust.json
and
.I trust.json.sig
(the site's delivery trust file),
.I deployment.json
and
.I deployment.json.sig
(one deployment naming this unit) and
.IB bundles/ label /
(1 to 16 release bundles,
.BR robodr\-bundle/1 ).
Anything else in it, a symlink or a special file anywhere refuses the
pack.
.PP
The media is never trusted and never used after the check: the documents
are read once, the trust file is accepted only as the unit's
.B trust anchor
or a root-signed rotation of it (pinned root fingerprints, the unit's own
site, unexpired), the deployment must carry the depot's signature and the
operator quorum of its ring, name this unit (a
.B lab
or
.B canary
deployment by unit id; a production ring by unit id or an enrolled group),
be for the unit's own ring (from the trust file's
.IR rings ,
else
.IR unit.ring ),
fit the ring's size and be newer than the accepted one, and every bundle
is copied bounded by its listed sizes while hashing and verified as the
copy (the verified-copy import of
.BR robodr\-release ).
.PP
Then, item by item, one switch per pass: the RPK must fit the unit
(architecture, platform, hardware, kind, floors, revocations, dependencies);
it is put into the content-addressed store and prepared next to the running
version; the install waits for the deployment's maintenance window, a local
operator's confirmation when the RPK requires one, and
.BR robodr\-agentd (8)'s
.B live
safety state, which must be
.B idle
or
.B safe_state_active
(read again immediately before the switch). The write-ahead intent and the
raised anti-rollback floor are written before the switch (a
.I current
symlink swap, or
.BR "dpkg \-\-install" ).
The RPK's health checks then decide: pass, installed; fail, automatic
rollback to the previous version at or above the floor, or, when there is
none, the unit stays on the failing version and a Critical finding asks an
operator to act. A
.B deb
package's
.BR Conflicts ", " Breaks ", " Replaces " and " Provides
may name only its own package (RoboDR's own pair excepted, from the
designated programme only). Right before the switch every path of the
.B .deb
is resolved through the target root's symlinks (merged-/usr aliases such
as
.IR "/lib \-> usr/lib" )
and refused when another package owns it, or when it lies below the
agent's own trees, RoboDR's install paths,
.IR /etc/dpkg ", " /etc/apt " or " /var/lib/dpkg ;
dpkg runs with
.B \-\-refuse\-all
(then only the force options the agent needs) and
.BR \-\-refuse\-overwrite ,
which override
.IR dpkg.cfg.d .
A recorded package changed outside the agent (apt, an operator) is
reported
.RB ( changed_outside )
and deb installs wait; the agent never downgrades it. After every
.B dpkg \-\-install
every other recorded
.B deb
package must still be installed at its recorded version, and its
.B dpkg \-\-verify
output and file list must be those it had right after its own install,
otherwise it is reinstalled and the switch fails.
.PP
.B deb
is for Debian and Ubuntu units only: on a unit without a dpkg database
.RI ( <dpkg_root>/var/lib/dpkg/status ,
an RPM-based host) a
.B deb
RPK is refused before anything is unpacked, and there is no
.B rpm
kind: RoboDR delivers no RPM packages (the other kinds work on any
unit). Every
.B dpkg \-\-install
runs with
.B ROBODR_DELIVERD_INSTALL=1
in its environment, which dpkg hands to the maintainer scripts. RoboDR's
own postinst then does not restart
.B robodr\-deliverd.service
(that would stop the agent waiting for dpkg, and with it dpkg and the
postinst): when RoboDR's own package is delivered, the agent finishes the
pass (health gate, commit or rollback) on its old binary, sees its
executable replaced on disk and exits 3; the unit
.RB ( RestartForceExitStatus=3 )
starts it again on the new one.
.PP
Every step is recorded in the agent's black box
.RI ( /var/lib/robodr/blackbox/deliverd ,
signed with the unit's black-box key) and reported as a
.B robot.deliver.*
finding in agentd's findings spool (contract
.B robodr.findings
2.2).
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B run
Loop every
.I interval_ms
until
.B SIGTERM
or
.B SIGINT
(handled between passes). An evidence or state write that fails stops the
daemon with status 1; the next start resumes the intent.
.TP
.B once
One pass; prints a JSON report
.RI ( imported ", " refused ", " installed ", " deferred ", "
.IR rolled_back ", " blocked ", " recovered ", " stale ).
.TP
.B check\-config
Validate the configuration and exit.
.TP
.B init\-anchor
Create the unit's trust anchor
.RI ( state_dir /trust\-anchor.json
and
.IR .sig )
from a trust file accepted under the pinned roots and the unit's site.
Once, at enrolment; refuses to overwrite an existing anchor (it moves
forward only by accepted rotations carried in media packs).
.TP
.B reanchor
Lift the lock the agent sets (no import, install or rollback until then)
when it cannot show that its state is current: its signed high-water
record
.RI ( blackbox.dir /highwater.json:
floors, deployment sequence, time floor, chain heads, the lock and a
generation counter noted in the black box and the state file) is older
than the black box or the state file says, is locked, or is missing or
does not verify while the state file or the black box exists. Accepts the
site's current trust file as the anchor, a rotation of it, or a new
anchor when none is left, but never one older than the newest version
the black box has seen; rebuilds the newest accepted deployment, the
chain heads, the time floor and the floors from the black box; takes the
trust file's signed
.B issued_at
as a time floor too and records the step. Stop the service first (the
journal is single-writer). The configured
.B blackbox.key
is usually a systemd credential that exists only while the service runs:
pass the key file itself with
.B \-\-key
.RI ( /etc/robodr/keys/agentd\-blackbox.key ;
a regular file of the invoking user without group or other permissions).
.TP
.B rekey
After the unit's black-box key was rotated (the journal and the
high-water record are signed with it): verify the journal and the record
with the old key, note the hand-over in the old journal, archive it as
.IR blackbox.dir .archived\-g N
and start a new journal and a new record, one generation newer, signed
with the new key and carrying the floors, the deployment sequence, the
chain heads, the time floor and any lock. Refused when anything does not
verify with the old key (re-anchor with
.B \-\-key
first). Stop the service first; both key files as for
.BR \-\-key .
Run it again to finish an interrupted run. Never delete the black-box
directory instead. Moving off a legacy HMAC key (old key HMAC, new key
Ed25519) needs
.B blackbox.allow_hmac
until it is done.
.TP
.B status
Print the state: installed versions, kept rollback targets, floors, the
deployment being worked on, the intent in progress, staleness.
.TP
.B confirm
Record a local operator's confirmation for package
.I NAME
of the deployment with digest
.I DIGEST
(see
.BR status )
when its RPK sets
.BR install.requires_operator_present .
Consumed by the install.
.TP
.B erase
Crypto-erase the unit's sealing keys (needs the
.B seal
section): the TPM's erase epoch moves on, so every key sealed to the unit
is dead; without a TPM the runtime volume's LUKS2 keyslots are wiped
.RB ( seal.device
or
.BR \-\-device ,
required). With a TPM a device's keyslots are wiped too. Then
.B seal.stop_units
are stopped,
.I runtime_root
is unmounted and
.B seal.mapper_name
closed, or suspended when busy. Reports
.B robot.seal.erased
(Critical; its text lists what was destroyed and the volume's state),
preceded by
.B robot.seal.tamper
for the trigger
.BR tamper .
Final: recovery is re-enrolment and re-delivery. Stop the service first;
.B \-\-key
as for
.BR reanchor .
.TP
.B pull
Fetch this unit's pack from the site depot over mutual TLS (the
.B depot
section, Sprint 14a): the depot's unit CA
.RB ( depot.ca_file )
is the only trust anchor, the client certificate is the one from
enrolment and its key is the unit identity key inside the TPM (signed
with
.BR tpm2_sign ;
the key never leaves the TPM). The pack goes to
.IR media_dir/depot\-lan
and is imported like a media pack (every signature, the deployment, the
unit's wrapped keys). A lease in the pack is installed.
.B run
pulls every pass when the section is set.
.TP
.B report
Send this unit's install report (installed items, health verdicts, TPM
clock and reset count), signed with the identity key, to the depot; with
.B \-\-out
write it for a media round trip
.RB ( "robodr\-depot report import" ).
A lease in the depot's answer is installed.
.B run
reports every 60 seconds when the section is set.
.TP
.B lease
Install a site lease file (Sprint 14b), checked against the lease key the
UWK was enrolled with. Sealed payloads of a unit enrolled with a lease
key unseal only while the lease is current in the TPM's own clock and
reset count; otherwise the item waits
.RB ( robot.seal.lease_expired ,
Medium) until the depot renews the lease.
.TP
.B tamper
The platform's side of the erase trigger socket
.RB ( erase.trigger_socket ):
send one trigger line. The agent accepts it only from root or a feeder
listed in
.BR erase.feeder_uids " / " feeder_gids
.RB ( SO_PEERCRED ;
anything else is journaled and ignored), plans the crypto-erase with the
responder and executes it when the live safety state is
.BR idle ", " safe_state_active " or " estop_active ;
in any other state, including unknown, it is deferred
.RB ( robot.seal.erase_deferred ,
Medium, once per state) and kept pending in the state file, across
restarts, until the state allows it. The state is read again immediately
before acting; the intent is journaled first. A
.B tamper
trigger reports
.B robot.seal.tamper
(Critical) at once.
.TP
.B safety\-state
Print, as JSON, the live safety state as the install gate sees it (the
.B safety
section of
.BR \-\-config ,
or its defaults: agentd's status socket, peer\-checked) and whether
agentd's heartbeat is fresh
.RB ( agentd_fresh ).
Exit 0 when the gateway and the executor may be restarted now
.RB ( idle " or " safe_state_active ),
1 otherwise.
.B install\-bundle.sh
uses it to gate package upgrades and for its post\-install health check
(OPERATIONS.md section 7).
.TP
.B verify\-minisign
Check the minisign signature
.RI ( FILE .minisig,
or
.BR \-\-sig )
of
.I FILE
against a minisign public key file with RoboDR's built\-in verifier and
print the trusted comment. Only prehashed signatures (the minisign
default, as releases are signed) are accepted; Ed25519 is verified
strictly and the trusted comment's signature is checked.
.B \-\-self\-test
runs the verifier's known\-answer test.
.B install\-bundle.sh
uses it instead of the
.B minisign
program once a release carrying it is installed, and then refuses
.BR ROBODR_ALLOW_UNSIGNED .
.SH SEALED PACKAGES
With the optional
.B seal
section
.RB ( seal_dir ", " runtime_root ", " volume_check
.RB "(" dm_crypt ", default, or " unchecked " for tests only), " tpm
.RB "(" tcti ", " tools_dir ", " srk ", " epoch_nv ", " floor_nv ", " timeout_ms ", " work_dir " (tmpfs)); absent for a unit without a TPM),"
.B device
(the LUKS2 partition; required without a TPM),
.B mapper_name
and
.B stop_units
(what erase closes and stops)
the agent installs sealed
.BR files ,
.B model
and
.B config
RPKs, and every
.B model
RPK, only below
.IR runtime_root /{files,model,config}/,
which must be a mounted dm-crypt mapping. The deployment's wrapped key for
this unit is unwrapped by its TPM (signed PCR policy, policy floor, erase
epoch;
.BR robodr\-seal (8))
only when a version is staged; each chunk is authenticated before it is
written. A TPM refusal or an erased unit is
.B robot.seal.unseal_failed
(High); a wrap that is not this unit's is
.B robot.deliver.refused
.RB ( unauthentic_key ).
The shipped unit reaches only
.I /dev/tpmrm0
.RB ( DevicePolicy=closed ", " DeviceAllow= ).
.SH CONFIGURATION
.I /etc/robodr/deliverd.json
.RB ( robodr\-deliverd\-config/1 ,
unknown members refused). Required:
.BR unit.id ,
.BR unit.site ,
.BR unit.model ,
.BR unit.platform ,
.B unit.arch
.RB ( amd64 " or " arm64 ),
.B trust.roots
(the delivery roots' fingerprints, pinned out of band) and
.B trust.clock
.RB ( trusted :
expiry on the system clock;
.BR untrusted :
expiry on the newest depot-signed time accepted, so a trust file or
deployment expired "by the depot's clock" is a staleness finding). The
rest has the defaults of
.IR /usr/share/doc/robodr/examples/deliverd.example.json :
install roots
.IR /opt/robodr\-delivered ,
.I /etc/robodr\-delivered
(the only place a
.B config
package writes) and
.I /etc/robodr\-delivered\-rules
(one symlink per rules package to its active version: point Pulsar's
.I rules_path
or a link below it there), probe UID range 1000..59999 (never a system
account), agentd's status socket
.I /run/robodr\-agentd/status.sock
(accepted only from the user
.BR robodr\-agentd " or root, checked with " SO_PEERCRED )
and spool, no
.B install.robodr_programme
(no deb RPK may touch RoboDR's own packages). With
.B trust.clock: untrusted
a maintenance window never opens.
.B pack_digest_max_mb
(default 4096) bounds the bytes hashed to recognise a media pack by its
content. Paths must be absolute and normalised; the state, media and
install trees must be disjoint.
.PP
Optional sections of Sprints 14a and 14b:
.B depot
.RB ( address ", " server_name ", " ca_file ", " cert_file ,
.BR deadline_s ", " max_pack_mb )
for LAN delivery
.RB ( deadline_s
bounds the connect, TLS handshake, request and answer head together; a
pack body gets
.B deadline_s
more plus one second per MiB);
.B erase
.RB ( trigger_socket ", " feeder_uids ", " feeder_gids )
for executed crypto-erase;
.B seal.anti_rollback_nv
(an NV counter index defined with
.BR "robodr\-seal counter define" )
moves the anti-rollback anchor into the TPM: the signed high-water record
carries the counter value it was written at, and the counter is
incremented after each write. A record behind the counter (a restored
disk) locks the agent; on the first start with the counter, the floors of
the 13b state file and record are migrated into it
.RB ( robodr.deliver.anchor_migrated
in the journal).
.SH HEALTH CHECKS
.B probe
argv are executed directly
.RB ( execve ,
no shell, no
.B PATH
lookup, environment
.BR PATH=/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin " and " LC_ALL=C ,
working directory /) as the probe user's UID and GID with supplementary
groups dropped, in a session and process group of their own, which is killed when the
timeout expires, under resource limits (CPU: the timeout plus one second;
.BR health.probe_max_memory_mb ", " probe_max_open_files ", " probe_max_processes ),
with the agent as their subreaper: any process a probe leaves behind,
even in a session of its own, is killed with its process group (only
groups outside the agent's session; until none is left, at most 10
seconds) and fails it; output beyond
.I max_output_bytes
is read and discarded. The probe user is resolved from the passwd file at
staging (never UID or GID 0, never
.BR root ", " robodr ", " robodr\-agentd ", " robodr\-exec ,
inside the configured range) and must resolve the same way at probe time.
.B service_active
runs
.BR "systemctl is\-active \-\-quiet" ;
.B file_present
needs a regular file;
.B ros2_node
cannot be evaluated on a unit and refuses the RPK. With
.B health.require_agentd_ok
(default) agentd's own health must be
.BR ok .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Refused or error (stderr says why).
.TP
.B 2
Usage or configuration error.
.TP
.B 3
.RB ( run
only) The agent's own executable was replaced on disk (a self-upgrade);
the pass in progress was finished. Start it again (the unit does).
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /var/lib/robodr/deliverd/
State
.RI ( state.json ),
trust anchor, content-addressed store, imported packs, operator
confirmations (0700 root).
.TP
.I /var/lib/robodr/deliverd\-media/
Where media packs are put (untrusted).
.TP
.I /var/lib/robodr/blackbox/deliverd/
The delivery black box (verify with
.BR "robodr\-blackbox verify" )
and the signed high-water record
.IR highwater.json .
.SH SYSTEMD UNIT
.B robodr\-deliverd.service
(installed disabled) runs as root with a bounded capability set, no
network, no access to
.IR /etc/robodr/keys ;
the black-box key arrives as
.IR /run/credentials/robodr\-deliverd.service/blackbox.key .
It must be an Ed25519 key: a legacy HMAC\-SHA256 key file is refused (at
start, by
.B reanchor
and by
.BR rekey )
unless the configuration sets
.B blackbox.allow_hmac
to
.B true
(default
.BR false ;
then a warning is printed).
Enable after commissioning and
.BR init\-anchor .
LAN delivery and the erase trigger socket need the drop-in example
.I /usr/share/doc/robodr/examples/robodr\-deliverd.service.d/50\-depot\-lan.conf.example
(TCP to the depot's address only; a runtime directory for the socket).
.SH LIMITS
Kinds
.BR oci\-image ", " robodr\-profile ", " os\-image
are refused, and sealed RPKs and models without a
.B seal
section. Without
.BR seal.anti_rollback_nv ,
floors live in the state file, the signed
high-water record and the black box only: root deleting the
black box together with the other two, or flooding it and putting an
older record back, is not detected (with it, the TPM counter detects
it). The safety state is
taken from agentd's user only; whoever controls root, that user or a
configured safety feeder can still claim
.BR idle .
Release signers are root-equivalent for
.B deb
packages: the unit's hardening does not contain approved content.
